  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRRS2024 FSM study IOM Bangladesh</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Rohingya crisis of 2017 has led to the establishment of the largest refugee camp with more than one million of inhabitants, located near Cox&apos;s Bazar, Bangladesh.In Camp 11, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) operates a Decentralised Wastewater Treatment System (DEWATS) to treat 4,000 litres of faecal sludge daily and the treated effluent is discharged into the ground through an infiltration bed. Nonetheless, the effluent can still present pathogens, representing a hazard for the local community, thereby the need of further treatment.An innovative technology, based on the heating of the DEWATS effluent through solar thermal energy, has been developed by Veolia Foundation to ensure the pasteurisation of the DEWATS effluent. This innovative technology is currently undergoing testing, with funding from ELRHA.The intern will support the study through testing of samples in both in Delft and in Cox&apos;s Bazar.</narrative>
  </description>
